WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:01.788
Carbon Tax.

00:00:01.738 --> 00:00:08.454
Canada's carbon pricing framework: what it costs, who pays, who's exempt, and whether the rebate covers the cost.

00:00:08.454 --> 00:00:16.409
This page presents both sides of the debate using official data from the Parliamentary Budget Officer, Environment Canada, and Statistics Canada.

00:00:16.409 --> 00:00:21.386
The carbon tax is one of the most politically divisive policies in Canadian history.

00:00:21.386 --> 00:00:24.545
The data is what matters — not the politics.

00:00:24.545 --> 00:00:26.215
The framework.

00:00:26.215 --> 00:00:30.954
Canada implemented a federal carbon pricing backstop in 2019.

00:00:30.954 --> 00:00:35.931
Provinces can adopt their own equivalent systems or fall under the federal backstop.

00:00:35.931 --> 00:00:41.511
The price started at twenty dollars per tonne of CO2 equivalent and has escalated annually.

00:00:41.511 --> 00:00:46.284
The consumer carbon levy was removed by the Carney government in 2025.

00:00:46.284 --> 00:00:50.011
The industrial Output-Based Pricing System continues.

00:00:50.011 --> 00:00:54.420
The PBO has published multiple analyses of net household impact.

00:00:54.420 --> 00:00:57.465
The numbers from the Parliamentary Budget Officer.

00:00:57.465 --> 00:01:04.374
The PBO found that most households in backstop provinces received more in rebates than they paid in direct carbon costs.

00:01:04.374 --> 00:01:16.534
However, when broader economic impacts are included — reduced GDP growth, investment effects, and indirect cost pass-throughs — the PBO found the net fiscal impact was negative for most households in most provinces.

00:01:16.534 --> 00:01:22.022
The difference between direct costs and total economic impact is the core of the policy debate.

00:01:22.022 --> 00:01:27.193
Both sides of the debate presented from official sources and published economic research.

00:01:27.193 --> 00:01:32.886
The case for: market-based mechanism, revenue-neutral design, emissions reduction signal.

00:01:32.886 --> 00:01:42.204
The case against: regressive impact on rural and northern communities, industrial exemptions undermine effectiveness, GDP reduction exceeds environmental benefit.

00:01:42.204 --> 00:01:44.874
This page does not take a position.

00:01:44.874 --> 00:01:48.090
The data is presented for the reader to evaluate.

00:01:48.090 --> 00:01:50.022
The policy pattern.

00:01:50.022 --> 00:02:08.488
The carbon tax debate illustrates a broader pattern documented across TENET5 investigations: policy is designed at the international level through forums like the WEF and UN climate conferences, implemented domestically with promises of revenue neutrality, and the distributional impact falls disproportionately on populations with the least political power.

00:02:08.488 --> 00:02:15.159
The Carney government removed the consumer levy while maintaining industrial pricing — the political response to public pressure.

00:02:15.159 --> 00:02:20.772
The question is whether the policy was ever about emissions or always about institutional alignment.
